1
00:00:00,350 --> 00:00:02,116
Lesson 11.1.

2
00:00:02,496 --> 00:00:06,123
THRML: simulating TSUs on GPUs.

3
00:00:07,250 --> 00:00:09,859
Before having a chip, one
must be able to program it.

4
00:00:10,239 --> 00:00:16,202
THRML is Extropic's library for simulating
probabilistic computers on GPUs.

5
00:00:16,582 --> 00:00:21,377
It is built on JAX and serves as a
scaffold for implementing block sampling

6
00:00:21,377 --> 00:00:26,683
algorithms on sparse, heterogeneous graphical
models, like those its hardware runs.

7
00:00:27,063 --> 00:00:31,526
Today we use it for real: we check
its exactness and measure its speed.

8
00:00:32,650 --> 00:00:37,070
The starting point is a
factorized energy-based model: the

9
00:00:37,070 --> 00:00:41,719
energy is a sum of factors, each
over a few nearby variables.

10
00:00:42,099 --> 00:00:45,393
Sampling uses Gibbs: each
variable is updated according

11
00:00:45,393 --> 00:00:48,328
to its distribution
conditioned on its neighbors.

12
00:00:48,708 --> 00:00:53,263
Since the conditional only depends on
the factors touching that variable,

13
00:00:53,263 --> 00:00:57,382
the update is local, exactly what
a chip can do with short wiring.

14
00:00:58,483 --> 00:01:01,394
THRML organizes this into pieces.

15
00:01:01,774 --> 00:01:07,346
Nodes represent variables of different
types: spins, categorical, continuous.

16
00:01:07,726 --> 00:01:11,750
Blocks group nodes that do not
interact with each other and can

17
00:01:11,750 --> 00:01:15,390
be updated at once, for example
the two colors of a grid.

18
00:01:15,770 --> 00:01:18,618
Factors define the energy, and conditional

19
00:01:18,618 --> 00:01:21,669
samplers say how to
update each type of node.

20
00:01:22,049 --> 00:01:25,964
A sampling program puts everything
together, and a schedule sets the

21
00:01:25,964 --> 00:01:29,533
warm-up, the number of samples
and the sweeps between samples.

22
00:01:30,650 --> 00:01:34,401
We check it with a four-by-four
grid, sixteen spins with

23
00:01:34,401 --> 00:01:38,085
random couplings and fields,
where we can enumerate all

24
00:01:38,085 --> 00:01:41,501
sixty-five thousand five
hundred thirty-six states.

25
00:01:41,881 --> 00:01:46,903
With twenty thousand chains and
two colors, after one sweep the

26
00:01:46,903 --> 00:01:51,686
maximum error in the magnetizations
is 0.4; after ten, 0.04;

27
00:01:51,686 --> 00:01:56,150
after thirty, 0.01, the
statistical floor of the chains.

28
00:01:56,530 --> 00:01:59,202
The sampler converges to
the correct distribution.

29
00:02:00,317 --> 00:02:01,444
Now the speed.

30
00:02:01,824 --> 00:02:05,598
We simulate a grid of two
hundred sixty-two thousand

31
00:02:05,598 --> 00:02:09,082
spins, the size of a Z1
chip, on a consumer GPU.

32
00:02:09,462 --> 00:02:12,218
We get between 5.8 and 7.5 flips per

33
00:02:12,218 --> 00:02:15,740
nanosecond, depending
on the number of chains.

34
00:02:16,120 --> 00:02:19,852
An FPGA that hard-wires the
problem achieves about sixty.

35
00:02:20,232 --> 00:02:23,532
And the Z1 specification, two
hundred sixty-nine thousand

36
00:02:23,532 --> 00:02:27,122
pbits at fifty megahertz, implies
about thirteen thousand five

37
00:02:27,122 --> 00:02:30,191
hundred, almost two thousand
times more than our GPU.

38
00:02:31,317 --> 00:02:35,074
THRML does not try to compete
in speed with the chip.

39
00:02:35,454 --> 00:02:38,839
It is for designing and debugging
algorithms before having

40
00:02:38,839 --> 00:02:42,282
hardware, for training energy-based
models with contrastive

41
00:02:42,282 --> 00:02:46,310
divergence, and for validating that
a program samples what it should.

42
00:02:46,690 --> 00:02:50,270
Its programs use the same
abstractions as the hardware:

43
00:02:50,270 --> 00:02:53,785
blocks, local conditionals
and schedules, so that what

44
00:02:53,785 --> 00:02:57,171
works in simulation can be
carried over to the chip.

45
00:02:58,283 --> 00:03:03,472
Use THRML to prototype block samplers,
train energy-based models with

46
00:03:03,472 --> 00:03:08,586
clamped and free phases, validate
first against exact enumeration on

47
00:03:08,586 --> 00:03:13,173
small graphs, and scale with
many chains and several devices.

48
00:03:14,283 --> 00:03:18,184
Common mistakes: putting
interacting nodes in the same block.

49
00:03:18,564 --> 00:03:20,756
Using few warm-up sweeps.

50
00:03:21,136 --> 00:03:24,181
Comparing flips per nanosecond
without declaring sizes.

51
00:03:24,561 --> 00:03:27,762
And skipping the exact
check on a small graph.

52
00:03:28,883 --> 00:03:34,077
In summary, THRML simulates on GPU
the block sampling the hardware

53
00:03:34,077 --> 00:03:38,955
runs, with nodes, blocks, factors,
conditionals and schedules.

54
00:03:39,335 --> 00:03:42,493
We verified it against exact
enumeration, with error

55
00:03:42,493 --> 00:03:45,226
at the statistical floor
after thirty sweeps.

56
00:03:45,606 --> 00:03:48,934
In speed, a consumer GPU
gives about seven flips per

57
00:03:48,934 --> 00:03:52,646
nanosecond, against thirteen
thousand five hundred implied

58
00:03:52,646 --> 00:03:56,165
by Z1: simulation is for
designing, not for production.

59
00:03:57,283 --> 00:04:02,033
To practice: explain why same-color
nodes are updated in parallel.

60
00:04:02,413 --> 00:04:04,096
Compute the statistical floor.

61
00:04:04,476 --> 00:04:06,870
Compute Z1's flips per nanosecond.

62
00:04:07,250 --> 00:04:09,707
And say what a sampling
schedule specifies.
